# SettingsRenderer Call Graph

The entry point is `src/betterdiscord/ui/settings.tsx`. In the first half of the file, note a few objects that are easy to confuse: line 31 creates `SettingsRenderer`, line 38 reads the drawer state at module scope, and only line 81 starts handling `DiscordModules.Layout`.

```ts
const SettingsRenderer = new class SettingsRenderer {

const drawerStates: Partial<Record<string, Record<string, boolean>>> = JsonStore.get("misc", "drawerStates") || {};

const layoutModuleRaw = DiscordModules.Layout;
const out: Partial<LayoutBuilder> = {};
```

The default value of `drawerStates` is an empty object; this is the initialization branch taken when no saved value can be read. In the layout section, `layoutModuleRaw` retains the original module, while `out` is the partially populated `Partial<LayoutBuilder>`. Later, you can also locate `match` (line 87), `outKey` (line 91), and `rootLayout` (line 110); continue by tracing these names to their assignment sites.

The file header also imports React, the settings store, `JsonStore`, and the webpack lookup utility. The method body of `SettingsRenderer` and the exact shape of `rootLayout` have not yet been extracted.

# Plural Call-Relationship Code-Reading Notes

The older notes for Plural start at `src/common/i18n.ts`. It was not split into a separate interface document at the time because we first needed to confirm whether it merely shared an entry point with the localization resources and formatting utilities or whether there was an actual ordering relationship.

The file header first shows four visible directions:

```ts
import Localizations from "@assets/locales";
import Default from "@assets/locales/en-us.json";
import {formatString, getNestedProp} from "@common/utils";
import Logger from "./logger";
```

The initial reading path was to follow the name of `Plural` back to the same file, then look further down for uses of `formatString` and `getNestedProp`. Read `Localizations` and `Default` first: they point to the locale directory and the default-language file, respectively, and must not be treated as a fixed input chain for Plural merely because they are both in the import section.

The working assumption recorded at the time was fairly rough: start with the utility functions and we would probably understand Plural. That assumption later proved insufficient; if the file contains a logging branch, the actual location of `Logger` should also be checked. Keep the entry point and sequence here so they can be traced back from the old call sites.

# Lazy Lookup Listener Wake-Up Proposal

At compatibility train startup, there is no clear next step for a lazy lookup that's waiting. The initial idea was to treat this as a scheduling problem: keep the request when a lookup misses, then retry those requests as more modules become searchable. This avoids putting each waiter in its own long-running loop.

## Options to try first

There are three candidate approaches:

- Stop on a miss and let the caller decide whether to look again;
- Keep querying until the caller gives up;
- Save the conditions that haven't matched yet, and check only potentially relevant waiters when a module arrives.

Leaning toward the third option. Waiters should retain their original matching conditions and callbacks, without making another copy of the module cache. When a module arrives, first evaluate it against the filters in effect at that point, then wake matching waiters; nonmatching waiters keep waiting. Remove a waiter only after its callback succeeds, so that if only some callbacks are processed for one arrival, the remaining requests aren't dropped as well.

The first draft also keeps filtering in the same flow, before wake-up. This was only to keep the first round of changes localized; we haven't yet confirmed whether a filter throwing an exception should affect one waiter or the whole batch. Issues related to declaration filtering are tracked separately in DOC-13; we won't set rules for them here.

## Checks to add

- Whether registering the same condition repeatedly should retain multiple callbacks;
- Whether the lookup entry point can still return a direct match when a module arrives before the listener is registered;
- Whether the remaining waiters can still be processed if one callback throws;
- Whether waiter removal timing can be kept consistent with ordinary lookups.

Prepare a narrowly scoped change; don't switch all webpack lookup entry points to the new calling pattern.

# Markdown Wrapper Compatibility Decision

In this compatibility wrap-up, two work phrases often appear side by side: adapt the markdown wrapper, and bind public data to an addon. Put them in the same record for now, but do not treat their being listed together as a confirmed implementation relationship.

The partial entry point currently available for inspection is `src/betterdiscord/ui/base/markdown.tsx`. The file header contains React, the Discord module entry point, and types related to markdown and components:

```ts
import React from "@modules/react";
import DiscordModules from "@modules/discordmodules";
import type {Rules, SimpleMarkdown} from "discord/modules";
import type {ComponentClass, PropsWithChildren} from "react";
```

The provisional approach is to keep two tracks separate. Continue from this file for the wrapper adaptation, looking at the component shape, rule types, and direct module dependencies; confirm separately what state the addon retains after the public call completes. Just because `DiscordModules` appears in the file header does not mean the data-binding issue should be assigned to the markdown wrapper layer.

Nor will we use an approach that only traces runtime imports. `Rules`, `SimpleMarkdown`, `ComponentClass` and `PropsWithChildren` are all part of this wrapper file's declaration surface; they do not indicate where the data comes from, but they affect what shape the wrapper can express.

Still needed: the wrapper component's actual usage points; the public data object on the addon side; whether the two work items are referenced in the same place.

# 2026.09 Build and Release Experiment

The build entry point is noted first at `scripts/build.ts`. Earlier investigation notes point package issues and the build call to this location; this time, only the entry point in `v_prev` is in scope, and caller speculation is not included.

The inputs for this run are divided into package metadata, locale input, and resources pending release. On inspecting the file header, I saw:

```ts
import Bun, {$} from "bun";
import path from "node:path";
import pkg from "../package.json";
import styleLoader from "bun-style-loader";
```

The script loads the package description, path handling, and style loading together; the existing record contains no actual artifact name or job output.

Record the experiment in two tracks. First, construct a case where the package checksum does not match the content to be packaged, and observe whether the build stops before release. Second, keep the same package metadata, change only the locale input, and check whether the locale and release assets remain in the release directory. Do not run the two tracks together, or it will be impossible to determine whether missing resources vary with the input. When recording the results, place the input values and directory names alongside the result.

This round only completed separating the entry point and variables; no pass/fail output has been obtained yet. Next time, run the second track first. Apart from the locale input, do not switch the source revision or change the package metadata; add the artifact name and stopping point here.
